::: {.doc-subject}

Relabel one or more columns using a function.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_boxhead.py#L160-L231){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
GT.cols_label_with(columns=None, fn=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

The `cols_label_with()` function allows for modification of column labels through a supplied
function. By default, the function will be invoked on all column labels but this can be limited
to a subset via the `columns` parameter.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[columns]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [SelectExpr]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The columns to target. Can either be a single column name or a series of column names
    provided in a list.

<code>[fn]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Callable[[str], str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   A function that accepts a column name as input and returns a label as output.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The GT object is returned. This is the same object that the method is called on so that we
    can facilitate method chaining.
:::

## Notes {.doc-notes}

GT always selects columns using their name in the underlying data. This means that a column's
label is purely for final presentation.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Let's use a subset of the `sp500` dataset to create a gt table.

::: {#b786ac16 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT, md
from great_tables.data import sp500

gt = GT(sp500.head())
gt
```

